s lobe, directly connecting the concept of an incision and the surgical approach to the gland’s location. [Data: Entities (4482, 4985, 5009); Relationships (11047)]\\n\\nFinally, the dataset states that lobectomy targets the thyroid lobe that contains a cancerous nodule, linking a focal lesion to organ-part resection rather than whole-gland removal in selected contexts. [Data: Entities (5009, 5020, 5052); Relationships (11046)]\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"summary\": \"A cancerous nodule is explicitly linked to thyroid cancer in the lobectomy context\",\n      \"explanation\": \"Within this community, a “cancerous nodule” is described as a cancer-containing lump in a thyroid lobe and is referenced as the reason for lobectomy (removal of the affected lobe). This positions the nodule as the immediate disease target driving surgical intervention. [Data: Entities (5052, 5009, 5020); Relationships (11046)]\\n\\nA relationship explicitly links thyroid cancer to the cancerous nodule as part of the lobectomy description, reinforcing the clinical framing that malignant nodules represent thyroid cancer pathology in this dataset’s context. [Data: Relationships (11053)]\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"summary\": \"Prior neck radiation history is a decision criterion influencing whether lobectomy is preferred\",\n      \"explanation\": \"The lobectomy description includes patient selection considerations and notes that absence of a history of prior radiation therapy to the neck supports lobectomy as a preferred option in appropriate cases. This makes prior radiation therapy a key contextual entity for surgical decision-making rather than a procedural step. [Data: Entities (5009, 5129); Relationships (11281)]\\n\\nBecause this factor is presented as a criterion in choosing lobectomy versus more extensive surgery, it functions as a risk/eligibility modifier in the pathway connecting diagnosis to operative planning. [Data: Entities (5129, 5009); Relationships (11281)]\"\n    },\n    {\n    